  "history": "NH 16 is the great east-coast highway of India, running from Kolkata down to Chennai close to the shore of the Bay of Bengal. Most of it was the famous old NH 5 before the 2010 renumbering, and it forms the eastern arm of the Golden Quadrilateral. The road follows the flat coastal plain between the Eastern Ghats and the sea, crossing the deltas of some of India's biggest rivers and linking a chain of major ports and industrial cities.",
  "facts": [
    "About 1,711 km long, forming the Kolkata–Chennai arm of the Golden Quadrilateral.",
    "Crosses three of India's great rivers — the Mahanadi near Cuttack, the Godavari at Rajahmundry and the Krishna at Vijayawada.",
    "Passes close to Chilika Lake in Odisha, India's largest coastal lagoon.",
    "Serves the port cities of Visakhapatnam and Chennai.",
    "For long stretches it runs beside the Howrah–Chennai railway line down the coast."
  ],
